        "bodyTr": "President Lee Jae-myung is reportedly considering Democratic Party members Park Gyun-taek and Yang Bu-nam as candidates for the Justice Minister position, which has remained vacant for two months. Beyond their shared background as first-term Honam representatives from the pro-Lee faction and former high court judges, these two lawmakers have notable commonalities. Park was recruited when Lee served as Democratic Party leader and worked as his legal advisor, acting as his counsel in the Baekhydong, Seongnam FC, and North Korea remittance cases. Yang also served as the party's legal committee chairman, managing the party's response to Lee's judicial risks. Both lawmakers belong to the Prosecution Cancellation Members Association and have consistently stated that if evidence tampering or false testimony induction is confirmed in Lee-related cases, prosecution cancellation is necessary. After the parliamentary audit ended, the Democratic Party began pushing forward with special investigation legislation to clarify alleged evidence tampering and false testimony induction that arose during past prosecution investigations. While a clause granting prosecution cancellation authority to the special investigation was omitted at Lee's request, it remains possible for the Justice Minister to direct the Prosecutor General to cancel prosecution in specific cases. In this context, if Lee appoints a close aide and attorney-turned-lawmaker who has defended his judicial interests to the ministerial position, he cannot escape criticism of abusing his presidential appointment power to resolve his own legal issues. The current administration's appointment of President Lee's defense attorneys to key positions strains credulity. The presidential chief of staff's brother-in-law worked as counsel in Lee-related cases, while the Cheong Wa Dae secretaries for civil service discipline, political affairs, and legal matters are all former attorneys. Former attorneys have also been appointed as head of the Legislative Counsel's Office and director of planning and coordination at the National Intelligence Service, and even the financial and diplomatic sectors have been filled with Lee's former defense counsel, including the Financial Supervisory Service governor, Korea Deposit Insurance Corporation president, and UN ambassador. While previous administrations appointed close associates to key positions, they at least understood public scrutiny. In light of Kim Seung-won's withdrawal as a former minister candidate who headed the prosecution cancellation association and the subsequent replacement of the chief of staff, will the administration really push forward another attorney-turned-candidate for Justice Minister? President Lee should appoint a politically neutral and professionally qualified candidate to restore judicial credibility.",
